skill: Pandas and NumPy

@ Pandas Basics | B
Which pandas structure represents a table with labeled rows and columns?
= DataFrame
- Series
- ndarray
- Panel
? A DataFrame is a two-dimensional labeled data structure. A Series is a single column.

@ Pandas Basics | B
Which method shows the first five rows of a DataFrame?
= head()
- top()
- first()
- start()
? head(n) returns the first n rows and defaults to 5.

@ Pandas Basics | I
How do you select the column named price from a DataFrame df?
= df["price"]
- df.select("price")
- df(price)
- df.get_column("price")
? Square brackets with the column name return that column as a Series.

@ Pandas Basics | A
What is the difference between loc and iloc?
= loc selects by label while iloc selects by integer position
- loc selects by position while iloc selects by label
- They are identical
- loc works only on columns
? Use df.loc[row_label, col_label] for labels and df.iloc[row_pos, col_pos] for positions.

@ Pandas Basics | A
What does df.merge(other, on="id", how="left") do?
= Keeps all rows of df and adds matching columns from other
- Keeps only rows that match in both tables
- Keeps all rows from both tables
- Appends other below df
? how="left" works like a SQL LEFT JOIN.

@ Data Cleaning with Pandas | I
Which expression counts the missing values in each column of df?
= df.isnull().sum()
- df.count_nulls()
- df.missing()
- df.nan()
? isnull() returns a boolean DataFrame and sum() adds the True values per column.

@ Data Cleaning with Pandas | I
Which call removes rows that contain any missing value?
= df.dropna()
- df.fillna()
- df.remove_na()
- df.drop_nulls()
? dropna() drops rows or columns with missing data. fillna() replaces missing values.

@ Data Cleaning with Pandas | I
Which call removes duplicate rows from a DataFrame?
= df.drop_duplicates()
- df.unique_rows()
- df.distinct()
- df.remove_dup()
? drop_duplicates() keeps the first occurrence of each duplicated row by default.

@ Data Cleaning with Pandas | I
Which call converts a column of date strings into datetime values?
= pd.to_datetime(df["date"])
- df["date"].toDate()
- pd.date(df["date"])
- df["date"].parse_dates()
? pd.to_datetime parses strings into datetime64 values so you can filter and group by date.

@ Aggregation and Grouping | I
Which expression gives the average salary for each department?
= df.groupby("dept")["salary"].mean()
- df.mean("dept")
- df.avg(dept, salary)
- df.aggregate(dept=mean)
? groupby splits the rows by department and mean() averages the salary column in each group.

@ NumPy Basics | B
What does np.array([1, 2, 3]) * 2 return?
= array([2, 4, 6])
- [1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3]
- An error
- array([1, 2, 3, 2])
? NumPy arrays support element-wise arithmetic, unlike Python lists.

@ NumPy Basics | I
What is the shape of np.zeros((3, 4))?
= (3, 4)
- (4, 3)
- (12,)
- (3,)
? The tuple passed to zeros gives rows and columns, so 3 rows and 4 columns.
